\section*{Circulant Hadamard conjecture}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Circulant Hadamard orders and Barker lengths}: The manuscript claims that real circulant Hadamard matrices occur only at orders one and four. Its further consequence restricts Barker sequences longer than one to the seven lengths $2,3,4,5,7,11,13$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Circulant Hadamard orders and Barker lengths}: The first row is written in an integral cyclic group ring, where orthogonality becomes a scalar norm equation. Cyclotomic coefficient divisibility first reduces the order to $4u^2$ with odd $u$. An alternating product of character evaluations over the prime components is then followed through descent, ruling out $u>1$; standard correlation reductions give the Barker consequence.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Circulant Hadamard orders and Barker lengths}: Real circulant Hadamard orders are exactly 1 and 4; a corollary classifies Barker lengths greater than one as 2,3,4,5,7,11,13.

This page records partial support. Complete resolution of the catalogue question is not established by this record.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 179 describes Lean coverage for circulant order classification and even Barker lengths; the odd Barker classification is not selected.

\textit{Circulant Hadamard orders and Barker lengths}: The manuscript links \texttt{CirculantHadamard.lean} (declaration \texttt{OAI.CirculantHadamard.exists\_iff\_order\_one\_or\_four}), \texttt{EvenBarker.lean}.

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

